camssa:00308c2d-45b5-4f69-b848-24887db43733 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The purpose of OCSP is not related to the long-term preservation of data/information/knowledge. Therefore,\r
this criterion is considered not applicable to this specification.\r
\r
OCSP reference:\r
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2560'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:152eae3c-c28c-43fb-9df5-5b53f2232abe .

camssa:0031d6c9-0648-46d8-bc1d-798824b25a82 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The once-only principle is an e-government concept that aims to ensure that citizens, institutions, and companies only have to provide certain standard information to the authorities and administrations once.\r
\r
HTTP is a protocol developed to exchange information over the internet environment. Thus, HTTP eases the implementation of once-only principle by means of allowing administration's stakeholders reduce interaction.    \r
                                                                           \r
See: http://www.toop.eu/sites/default/files/D21_Federated_OOP_Architecture.pdf """@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:c3b228f4-3445-4450-a812-6630d9532e2b .

camssa:00365ea6-fc11-41b2-a212-08d952c32727 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The XACML specification plays a significant role in protecting information against unauthorised changes through its detailed access control policies and enforcement mechanisms. However, XACML does not directly handle all aspects of data protection against unauthorised changes.

XACML OASIS reference:
https://docs.oasis-open.org/xacml/3.0/xacml-3.0-core-spec-os-en.html'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4ad0b478-a4df-4cc2-8e54-2cfee82ec828 .

