    cav:value "1" .

camssa:02a5b0d2-0eea-4b95-872f-258df9bae8ee a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'There are two Member States recommending OData in their catalogues of recommended specifications. These Member States are The Netherlands and France.

Netherlands Catalogue reference:
https://www.forumstandaardisatie.nl/open-standaarden/aanbevolen

France catalogue reference:
https://www.numerique.gouv.fr/uploads/Referentiel_General_Interoperabilite_V2.pdf

CAMSS List of Standards:
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/common-assessment-method-standards-and-specifications-camss/camss-list-standards'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:7e763c37-d5c9-4267-982d-c90d0d592857 .

camssa:02b0041e-5ce5-47ca-96fe-43219e51abf3 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'YAML can be customized by adjusting key-value pairs, lists, and nested structures. Moreover, settings can be configured, and data structures can be defined and constructed.

YAML specification:
https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#31-processes'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:b3edb502-1fc3-45da-80c1-c23e061a682b .

camssa:02b1c1b9-8070-4adc-9c7c-24fbbaf012c6 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'OData.org provides a OData Service Validation Tool for free to validate OData implementations.

OData Service Validation Tool:
https://www.odata.org/blog/odata-service-validation-tool/'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:072058a2-e4cc-4453-91d9-ce9ada58b414 .

camssa:02b22929-6fc4-4a3f-8dc6-8cb3fe7639bf a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """XML is used to encode all communications to web services. Therefore, it fosters the availability of public administrations interfaces.\r
\r
Additionally, the benefit of using XML is that web services are not tied to any one operating system or programming.\r
\r
See:\r
https://esentool.ted.europa.eu/ \r
"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:00b7eb46-e19c-445b-b50f-0c32631d49b8 .

camssa:02b2eb83-c2d3-4eac-8a72-2af8760c1dac a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-9420538a5a92af199b492414dd056ab7134e456f005895261b1a5b15a3f2814e ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:02b5f712-7a54-4e57-8a57-4e97596955d3 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The protocol for remote digital signature creation can be domain-agnostic as it can be applicable across various industries and use cases. Standards for digital signatures have generally been developed for a long time considering solutions tailored to the characteristics of devices, therefore they could be implemented and/or used in any domain.

ETSI TS 119 432: https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/119400_119499/119432/01.02.01_60/ts_119432v010201p.pdf'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:7dff3c55-7621-4c00-a0f2-a097b22d402f .

camssa:02b74368-e390-4ba3-ada6-37ddf23d89ae a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Verifiable Credentials is developed by W3C, a standard development organisation based in the US. Moreover, Verifiable Credentials specification does not appear in any of the main European standard development bodies, therefore, the specification is not a European standard.\r
\r
CEN-CENELEC homepage: \r
https://www.cencenelec.eu/\r
\r
ICT technical specifications:\r
https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/single-market/european-standards/ict-standardisation/ict-technical-specifications_en'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:62c67ed5-8656-40a5-be42-40a0fbcb24c8 .

camssa:02b8eb73-0fc7-4107-9caf-ca8fb4077c32 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-fef5941bab92019b7692f8544de94715a42ed9ff76d89cbec4fb6f9821611925 ;
    cav:value "2" .

camssa:02bcd2d8-3da5-4a15-aeb0-4c8b48d7585d a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'OData promotes the sharing of data and results, and begs for its approval as an ISO/IEC Standard to help accelerate the specification adoption throughout the international community, particularly within government agencies. In this sense, the specification encourages both the creation of communities and the sharing of their data and results on national platforms.\r
\r
OData referenece, data exchange:\r
https://www.oasis-open.org/news/pr/iso-iec-jtc-1-approves-oasis-odata-standard-for-open-data-exchange/'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:e75c3d2e-7d19-4432-9573-bb71f3f851e0 .

camssa:02bfb37a-bb91-490f-8c9a-f8fbc2e956ba a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The last version of the specification was released in 2003 by ETSI’s Technical Committee Electronic Signatures and Infrastructures (ESI). ASiC Baseline profile is linked to the development of ASiC, evolving at the same time. As well, is worth to note that all ETSI standards are focused on market needs ensuring its market acceptance. \r
Moreover, its selection in European initiatives demonstrates its maturity for the development of products and services. """@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:ac1bf2be-8331-4082-8187-be0b75709386 .

camssa:02c119ef-4d3c-4138-a73e-9d82c1ea611d a cav:Score,
