camssa:04addabb-d0d4-411d-80a4-23cdac2e8ccd a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Although the Indexed Database API is addressed to the storage of information, it can also contribute to enabling certain aspects of data protection from unknown sources. In this context, the Indexed Database API follows a same-origin policy. While you can access stored data within a domain, you cannot access data across different domains. This helps to reduce unwanted user access.

Indexed Database API:
https://www.w3.org/TR/IndexedDB/

Indexed Dataase API same-origin policy:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/IndexedDB_API/Basic_Terminology'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:5cb9adb5-605a-43dc-8d77-18f925ac1845 .

camssa:04b0bd5a-5e9d-4ac9-acb6-8b52e0d5cfae a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'DCAT-AP, as a cross-border and cross-sector vocabulary that increases government-to-government reuse of data and therefore can prevent citizens or businesses from providing standard information several times. Like all common vocabularies, DCAT-AP facilitates the implementation of the once-only principle. \r
\r
DCAT-AP - a key for European data publishers to reach the data consumers and create a European open data ecosystem:                                                                                                                                      \r
https://ec.europa.eu/isa2/dcat-ap-key-european-data-publishers-reach-data-consumers-and-create-european-open-data-ecosystem_en\r
\r
DCAT-AP Specification:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/semantic-interoperability-community-semic/solution/dcat-application-profile-data-portals-europe/release/200'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:d3922e01-14e9-42a5-831d-c0c149743014 .

camssa:04b69164-d547-4712-b1a0-de5a6f0618dc a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'IETF has a formal review and approval so that all the relevant stakeholders can formally appeal or raise objections to the development and approval of specifications.\r
Each distinct version of an Internet standards-related specification is published as part of the "Request for Comments" (RFC) document series. This archival series is the official publication channel for Internet standards documents and other publications.\r
During the development of a specification, draft versions of the document are made available for informal review and comment by placing them in the IETF's "Internet-Drafts" directory, which is replicated on a number of Internet hosts. This makes an evolving working document readily available to a wide audience, facilitating the process of review and revision.\r
\r
Standard process IETF:\r
https://www.ietf.org/standards/process/\r
\r
Internet Best Current Practices IETF:\r
https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2026'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:d1d8aef0-820d-4e2a-b699-df84e08373f1 .

camssa:04b8ae39-f58d-41ed-a51c-617eb404bde0 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-7d12b846c487e7201dbcf395866b5abc0bb5fa040b1f519127b6a5f56d447257 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:04b9c8cc-076c-42d3-96cd-d15397b4b7a0 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """FTP allows public administration to exchange information and therefore to avoid requesting standard information to citizens or businesses several times. \r
\r
FTP is used by MAGA, an initiative from the Flemish Region of Belgium  consisting in a common platform for data reuse between administrations.\r
\r
See: \r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/solution/magda-platform/about\r
"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:51191ac3-4a71-487a-a931-d76b6c24d29e .

