camssa:105d9060-27c9-4f32-91a1-cae60aff4d38 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'IEEE Std 802.3-2022 can enable data portability between systems and applications supporting European public services. Its flexibility, scalability, and backward compatibility promote data sharing and interoperability. 

IEEE Std 802.3-2022 Reference:
https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9844436'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:5b9f8c94-c6f5-4812-ac07-33d11a53362c .

camssa:10641f83-bc9c-4edd-8ccc-a3dbeeae24b1 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The specification was carried out in 2001 and has been developed and maintained by OASIS. It has suffered 1 minor update (SAML 1.1) and a major one (SAML 2.0). This fact demonstrates the maturity of the specification that is being assessed. Moreover, it is also implemented by IT vendors and suppliers having a market dominance. For instance, SAML 2.0 is implemented by Microsoft for the Microsoft Azure Active Directory and by Amazon for Amazon Web Services. Therefore, it is demonstrated that SAML 2.0 has enough maturity and market acceptance for its use in the development of products and services.\r
\r
SAML 2.0 Reference:\r
http://docs.oasis-open.org/security/saml/Post2.0/sstc-saml-tech-overview-2.0.html\r
\r
Microsoft Azure Active using SAML 2.0 Reference: \r
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/active-directory/develop/single-sign-on-saml-protocol'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:974d7acb-bf8d-4c26-b194-638adc29d2e2 .

camssa:106515f3-8e07-4540-a5cb-054bdb37fdc0 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Slovakia and Portugal are the only European Member States that recommend the specification in their ICT National Catalogues.\r
\r
CAMSS List of Standards Reference:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/common-assessment-method-standards-and-specifications-camss/camss-list-standards'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:40a7e0a3-863c-4b57-8765-5aab44b312d4 .

camssa:10718127-b94e-4915-b307-8d8e9847c8d9 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The specification is a widely used de-facto standard. It was launched for the first time in 1998 by engineers from Microsoft. Other companies with a wide market share are contributed to the development of the specification. Additionally, SOAP is developed and maintained by W3C and its working group. Thus, it is sufficiently mature for the development of products and services.\r
\r
SOAP Reference:\r
https://www.w3.org/TR/soap12/  '"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:344e2555-b1f9-483a-8922-9a091e50d92f .

camssa:1071d680-05c8-4a21-a3e5-c21607bb54b4 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'HTTPS itself does not directly address the specific accessibility needs of users with disabilities, it is an essential component for providing secure and accessible online services. Nonetheless, it can contribute to the enabling of e-accessibility. By establishing a secure foundation upon which they can build and ensure inclusive e-accessibility for all users, regardless of their abilities.\r
\r
HTTPS specification:\r
https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2818'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:3b0318e1-e172-4966-914c-e53e72c67300 .

