        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-9694ffda6be83e507b6d3395cb411581d91419db0107726873a0a9018eab6a54 ;
    cav:value "20" .

camssa:10e1f4f3-7e3f-411b-8fbc-9700ff95b997 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'ISO 19941 was released in 2017 and a second major release (ISO/IEC AWI 19941) is under development. While all stages of the life cycle of the specification are public, its details are not.\r
\r
ISO 19941: https://www.iso.org/standard/66639.html\r
'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:168e5f7e-7b8b-445b-93d8-a86116cfc357 .

camssa:10e87ae3-83f4-44f0-88e4-36b50924d5d0 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The specification can be used as a global ledger by any domain. It can capture a broad set of business\r
transaction related data e.g. accounting and taxation related details, parties involved, products or services\r
traded. From the beginning of the development of XBRL, the recognition that financial reporting was vitally\r
important but not meant to be the complete scope of XBRL led to the rebranding of its first name, XFRML, to\r
XBRL. XBRL GL from its inception was designed to be a representation of detailed data across all business\r
reporting domains. XBRL participants include financial regulators, sustainability reporting groups, and any\r
other area of interest to business reporting stakeholders. XBRL GL was designed to represent holistically\r
any area of business reporting, quantitative and qualitative data, in any level of detail from source events\r
and business documents to more aggregated and summarized information, using a single, holistic, generic\r
and global specification.'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:e61c3ebf-c8b9-4c4e-ac0c-dbf50d7fb7b0 .

camssa:10ee9796-3ebe-4d5e-ba9c-a6d54eca8c55 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-d520eae57650560dd54723cdcdd166243c9a0b1458a4afdc331175b5c1cada7e ;
