camssa:22312efd-e3c0-4f69-91d5-3dbf4a80af1d a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'TLS 1.3 reduces the risk of unauthorised access and data breaches by guaranteeing that all post-handshake data is encrypted and visible only to the communicating endpoints. This is achieved through the secure encryption negotiated during the handshake. Server authentication is mandatory, while client authentication, using mechanisms like client certificates, is optional. 

TLS 1.3 Cryptographic Computations Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#section-7'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:bb072071-b2b0-49d3-90c8-7234f16aa44d .

camssa:223490a1-7d69-43c2-ac16-22bf97d19ecf a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """According to the 5 star Open Data model suggested by Tim Berners-Lee about how to publish linked data, the second star corresponds to make data available as structured data, an area of application of XML. \r
\r
Additionally, RDF is a W3C specification that defines XML syntax for RDF, the de facto standard for publishing Open Data. \r
\r
See:\r
https://5stardata.info/en/\r
https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf-syntax-grammar/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:e49925c6-8deb-4317-8f7d-96c596f76d56 .

camssa:22364748-dd50-4fff-9a1f-01ddaad10e53 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """XHTML is a business domain agnostic specification that can be reused in a cross-domain way.\r
\r
XHTML W3C reference:\r
https://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:5aa05c44-a466-43b6-b007-46ad5b52b828 .

