camssa:37e0a249-e043-4547-862b-a21deb10db57 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'As an Internet Protocol, IPv4 has been adopted by many countries and member states. It is the most widely used protocol for data exchange over the internet. Moreover, it enables the establishment of IP connectivity to a network by assigning an IP address to an interface, which helps to enhance administrative procedures in a comprehensible manner.

IPv4 IETF:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791

IPv4 interfaces:
https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/ios-xml/ios/ipaddr_ipv4/configuration/xe-3s/ipv4-xe-3s-book/configuring_ipv4_addresses.html#GUID-3D93A3CD-26A6-4128-9573-8A545064F899'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4fba1787-ad95-4151-a6c3-e0f4d6feeb22 .

camssa:37e22b31-a766-4254-96db-a0a314b722db a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) was established by the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) in 2001. The Rijndael submission by Daemen and Rijmen won an open competition organized by NIST in 2000. Then, in 2001, the submission was standardised as AES. However, the first version of the standard began to be discussed in 1997, although its original name was not AES. 
Since then, all releases have been published. And since its first version, one minor release addressing some formal aspects of the standard document has been launched.

NIST AES Reference:
https://www.nist.gov/publications/review-advanced-encryption-standard

AES Standard Reference:
https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/197/final'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:59f59219-b020-46f7-9440-00f484a33585 .

camssa:37e25889-ed08-46a0-abee-df8acc0af54d a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-51782b9aa4b92f4aba322a549b9d3972e304ca68f9353073c29add782502d910 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:37e41b69-b52d-47d6-ab10-78943e9b214e a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """By allowing electronic signatures, the specification helps to avoid the administrative burden related to the non-digital communications.\r
\r
XAdES ISO reference:\r
https://www.iso.org/standard/56025.html\r
\r
XAdES ETSI reference:\r
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/101900_101999/101903/01.04.02_60/ts_101903v010402p.pdf"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4e2766e3-fa61-4b1a-a8b3-849f8d0fc2dd .

camssa:37ea314a-558d-4a44-a073-e0324afbe560 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """All the IETF standards are free and open technical specification, built on IETF standards and licenses from the Open Web Foundation._x000D_\r
_x000D_\r
See:_x000D_\r
https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6749.txt  """@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:affb129d-d3db-463d-8be8-b6b7b0f1b0d4 .

camssa:37ec9e62-08a1-43d0-9643-dad6f430a7d9 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-9b3cb1ef128e2a87386258a5812c05b520024e6e4a404d1f1d6f0e35707e8a7b ;
    cav:value "80" .

camssa:37ee4fef-1cdb-4a99-bc9c-b33e46f9e20e a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Extesion mechanisms are provided by ETSI TS 119 512. For example, there can be found preservation-specific extensions for ASiC-related components (see section 5.5) and extensions for other preservation object formats (see section 5.6)\r
\r
ETSI TS 119 512 specification:\r
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/119500_119599/119512/01.01.02_60/ts_119512v010102p.pdf    '"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:7452cc04-ff0a-4370-abd1-80d432526994 .

camssa:38035567-317d-4acc-9767-1e5f4355cc27 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'XACML is developed by OASIS which is based in the Europe. Therefore, the specification can be regarded 
as a European Standard.

OASIS reference:
https://www.oasis-open.org/org/'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:a6d15f4e-5af3-4f41-bd3c-9174d31c0f5e .

camssa:3809046d-87fe-4c90-896d-51dff97f5650 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-cc272cd8a2e3e9c5e4edaa5ac7d59735c377a9db89d6958335a2cf0538761ab3 ;
    cav:value "100" .

