        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-3b3b2febe9a7e3ad44bdefc47b0bbcafc7a2e677e9570d6ad822efbe1f7d41e2 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:3a16f2cb-28ca-41ad-8ddb-ec1bf7e4107e a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The Core Location Vocabulary allows managing cross-border repositories of public services and organisations. So, it fosters data portability between systems and applications supporting the implementation and evolution of European public services.\r
\r
CLV Joinup reference:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/solution/e-government-core-vocabularies/core-location-vocabulary"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:424bde46-199b-4d3c-904d-b77a617dd5fd .

camssa:3a1be222-592d-406f-a662-06178fba17e3 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The XACML specification provides a robust framework for implementing access control mechanisms, like 
fine-grained access control by enabling policies that specify precise conditions under which access is granted or denied. Moreover, XACML enables organisations to enforce secure and consistent access control across their applications and resources.

XACML OASIS reference:
https://docs.oasis-open.org/xacml/3.0/xacml-3.0-core-spec-os-en.html'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:95c43009-e584-4098-be11-549fc2706553 .

camssa:3a1c7d93-129f-44b4-b6bc-33b6780c0c74 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """"Schema.org contains a specific vocabulary that can be used in applications that publish, discover or integrate different types of data. That is, not only does it provide schemas for describing collections of structured data, but also the specification's capability complements many other data-related formats and standards which facilitates organisational interoperability agreements.\r
\r
Schema.org reference, Data and Datasets overview section:\r
https://schema.org/docs/data-and-datasets.html\""""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:643cc10e-5be0-471f-af7d-20679a3004ec .

camssa:3a1d4615-fd93-42c9-9074-a9992185e73c a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The rules on European standardisation allow the European Commission to identify information and communication technology (ICT) technical specifications - that are not national, European or international standards - to be eligible for referencing in public procurement. This allows public authorities to make use of the full range of specifications when buying IT hardware, software and services, allowing for more competition in the field and reducing the risk of lock-in to proprietary systems.\r
\r
After being evaluated compliant with the regulation on standardisation 1025/2012, XML has been identified by Commission Implementing Decision. During the evaluation process, all the Member States are invited to share their doubts. The positive evaluation of XML and its identification is considered an interoperability agreement.\r
\r
See:\r
https://ec.europa.eu/growth/industry/policy/ict-standardisation/ict-technical-specifications_en"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:5599dc14-2053-49bc-ae98-e700149098b4 .

