camssa:460a5cfa-a66f-463b-8ddf-57bd29ffa364 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'HTTP specification does offer flexibility in how developers can implement the protocol. The use of terms like "MAY," "SHOULD," and "OPTIONAL" signals that not all features or behaviors are mandatory.

HTTP /1.1 RFC 2616 Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2616.html

HTTP/1.1 Semantics, RFC 9110 Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:b139f0ef-d984-4fe4-87b3-2ef207c67239 .

camssa:461308dc-b077-497a-a803-7cc53df32bf3 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'ETSI TS 119 432 was created as a support for electronic signatures and electronic seals according to the trust services for electronic transactions in the internal market and repealing Directive 1999/93/EC (the eIDAS Regulation). The eIDAS Regulation provides for the interoperability of national eID schemes among EU member states. This requires the development of a technology-neutral framework that does not favour any particular technical solution for eID implementation.

ETSI TS 119 432: https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/119400_119499/119432/01.02.01_60/ts_119432v010201p.pdf

eIDAS Regulation: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/eidas-regulation'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:ef73dbd1-4028-4d90-b6c8-c030b271f4cc .

camssa:46201661-a62c-42e1-be5c-aad511355b65 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'ETSI TS 119 511 is part of the Electronic Signatures and Infrastructure (ESI) family of specifications and is\r
dependent on some of the protocols and semantics they convey, therefore, this specification is not\r
technology agnostic.\r
\r
ETSI TS 119 511 specification:\r
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/119500_119599/119511/01.01.01_60/ts_119511v010101p.pdf'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:9ecf85c9-1fc1-431f-a91c-bd2ebc2ca22d .

camssa:462983af-b897-497b-97fa-c2a1130e84b2 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-b37687b8bb3dbd4c6f96969358cf5d70900d1ccd01402cea7668da6303bec4b4 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:462c581d-c8e5-4f5f-8c72-36e76719bbfe a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-a6fa642bc8eceff2b1d333dc1123748399264c028fde2fd78308f0a0209ae21b ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:462ec9b8-4c49-4491-9a0a-0f95b70cd022 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """LDAP is an application protocol for accessing and maintaining distributed directory information services over an Internet Protocol network, which defines the authentication user schema which allows and fosters its reuse in a cross-sector manner. However, it is not defining a data model. \r
\r
LDAP IETF reference:\r
https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4511"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:3dfafb70-b89a-4ea6-9f1f-f651e8008bad .

camssa:4630cd22-bfa8-4b8a-b1b2-f2a22eb345fb a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """"CPOV contriubutes to enabling exposure of interfaces in many ways. We can highlight link data from public organizations to other data sets, enable the creation of interoperable catalogues of public organisation in 
Europe and beyond, browse public organizations by its function, and keep track of the evolution of public organizations. Therefore, it improves the interoperability and reusability of data, allowing organisations to develop API's and web sevices that provide access to this information effectively.

CPOV Joinup reference:
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/semantic-interoperability-community-semic/solution/core-public-organisation-vocabulary/distribution/core-public-organisation-vocabulary-version-100-pdf\""""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:bf79eb82-c54a-4546-9347-36c51f1158fc .

camssa:46322e80-f0b4-4f59-ad69-647e516f5486 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-497a81dae06c1d53b4cfe96c1f998f6f65d46354d68a83d29a2950c984fc1166 ;
    cav:value "100" .

