        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-17bea234285e6e633e4e21105358610ad5f5f0eaaf82f4bd50cb64ec11538984 ;
    cav:value "1" .

camssa:5a7c70bb-9805-4e5f-b298-acc1dfdc90d8 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The Extensible Forms Description Language (XFDL) specification has its origins in the Universal Forms Description Language (UFDL) specification. This initial specification was developed from 1993 to 1998 by the UWI.com organisation. In this context, the XFDL specification is the result of creating an XML syntax for the UFDL, thereby enabling the expression of powerful, complex forms in a syntax that promotes application interoperability and adherence to global Internet standards.
In addition, there is a discontinued GitHub repository for the XFDL specification, where some information about the specification, such as its main page, can be found. However, there are still some niche software applications that continue using this specification for creating digital signatures or complex, legally binding forms with high precision.

Discontinued Github page for XFDL:
https://w3c.github.io/Unitas/?s=XFDL&v=19980902

Extensible Forms Description Language reference:
https://www.w3.org/TR/NOTE-XFDL'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:02c119ef-4d3c-4138-a73e-9d82c1ea611d .

camssa:5a8202e2-614a-4fe4-818e-ed54e92a4ad6 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-9c03a344765a6b8da57c4a7d40647791b737e3e1feecc14e3d59f2c69bdb246b ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:5a8292d4-3e64-4a04-b04b-e6027e739f4c a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The purpose of ISO 639-2 is not related to reduce administrative burden. The specification is intended to serve as a code to identify a language name. Therefore, this criterion is considered not applicable to this specification.\r
\r
ISO 369-2 FAQs:\r
https://www.loc.gov/standards/iso639-2/faq.html#7'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:3cacaf0d-bde5-41f4-bc44-dab0b34a38bd .

camssa:5a86191b-dd56-41dc-a0fd-7256c7b52232 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'There are 3 Member States recommending ISO 639-2 in their ICT National Catalogues. These Member States are Ireland, Norway and Spain.\r
\r
National catalogue Ireland:\r
https://data.gov.ie/pages/opendatatechnicalframework#recommended-standards-for-open-data\r
\r
National catalogue Norway, forth drop-down tap:\r
https://www.digdir.no/digitale-felleslosninger/nettlesarbaserte-tenester/1491\r
\r
National catalogue Spain:\r
https://administracionelectronica.gob.es/pae_Home/pae_Estrategias/pae_Interoperabilidad_Inicio/pae_Normas_tecnicas_de_interoperabilidad.html#CATALOGOESTANDARES'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:2266a9ac-6fba-4d3f-9df2-e35c05f8b5e0 .

camssa:5a8ecb4d-2bc8-48e8-9e96-605d4dc89dfc a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement "The fact that the specification has been widely adopted and the strong interest by different organisations and initiatives demonstrates that the specification has sufficient completeness and consistency for the use and development of products and services required to gather or exchange wheat-related data. "@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:29c403f2-0abe-4638-8598-a9459f961b1c .

camssa:5a8fdc59-88db-4d50-9eac-567359355a30 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """FTP has been widely used for the exchange of data in the past years and  has been supported for long time by most of the web browsers. \r
\r
However FTP is beginning to be considered obsolete by major browser. For instance Google Chrome, a browser having market dominance over the market deprecated the support for FTP and states that ¨Usage of FTP in the browser is sufficiently low that it is no longer viable to invest in improving the existing FTP client¨.\r
\r
See: \r
https://www.chromestatus.com/feature/6246151319715840"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:dd2be2d7-5774-44b9-9896-5b7a10184132 .

