    cav:value "100" .

camssa:6d51d869-4b53-494f-97bd-68672c3c7999 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """DCAT is a domain-agnostic specification that can be reused for the description of any catalogue of dataset independently from the business domain addressed.\r
\r
For DCAT users having business-specific requirements, the specification is generally extended (e.g. StatDCAT-AP for statistical data)"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:5e8195d8-9607-44ea-a3f3-74e12bb9d15b .

camssa:6d52ed3a-258d-4bc8-a1aa-2955478162dd a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-5a7323988b5d96da801e2abd4d95248af758fe9adbbafd09dd76114629567ff4 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:6d58a8b8-312b-4f9d-affb-0ba5ea4c9908 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """"After conducting a thorough search, no documents or studies were found that assess XHTML in terms of its
effectiveness. However, it is important to note that HTML5 now fulfills many of the same functions as
XHTML, leading to a decline in XHTML's usage. There is a good amount of research, studies, and resources
available about HTML5, demonstrating its value in supporting modern and more efficient web environments.

XHTML W3C reference:
https://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/

HTML5 W3C Reference:
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/

HTML5 Research Reference:
https://www.researchgate.net/publication/271329059_RESEARCH_ON_HTML5_DEVELOPMENT\""""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:6629042b-e6a2-4629-bd88-ce55d8f3f2ea .

camssa:6d5ed6ed-2a4e-4cb9-b157-662e9fce7e63 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The ebXML specification, as an enabler of electronic data interchange (EDI) functionalities over the Internet, has a wide-spread use in both private and public sectors. For instance, several initiatives in Europe have adopted the Universal Business Language (UBL), which is designed to be agnostic with respect to any EDI infrastructure and mapped to the ebXML core components, which proves the degree of usefulness of the specification and its suitability to be used in the development of digital solutions/services (see Joinup OASIS UBL reference).\r
\r
Joinup OASIS UBL reference:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/eprocurement/solution/oasis-universal-business-language-ubl/about\r
\r
ubl.xml.org reference:\r
http://ubl.xml.org/ebXML'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:792fc915-c7bc-4b9c-a9f8-878912faa2e3 .

camssa:6d5fa806-18c1-4f8b-8ecb-2164057201a1 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """SOAP 1.2 is associated to an EIRA ABB in the EIRA Library Of Interoperability Specifications (ELIS). More specifically, SOAP 1.2 can define the interoperability aspects of the "Machine to Machine Interface", "Data Exchange Component" and, “Data Exchange Service” ABBs from the EIRA Technical Infrastructure view.\r
\r
See:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/release/eira-library-interoperability-specifications-elis/v100-beta"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:a0cfb60c-5118-46f1-9819-1ad3f787ff8d .

camssa:6d605097-e35f-4efc-8f9e-4b4623a00148 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """HTML is included in 4 national catalogues of recommended specifications. They belong to Estonia, the Netherlands, Spain and Sweden.\r
The National Interoperability Framework (NIF) of these Member States is fully aligned with at least 4 out of 5 sections of the European Interoperability Framework (EIF) according to the National Interoperability Framework Observatory (NIFO) factsheets.\r
\r
CAMSS List of Standards:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/common-assessment-method-standards-and-specifications-camss/camss-list-standards\r
\r
NIFO factsheets:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/national-interoperability-framework-observatory-nifo/nifo-factsheets"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:ead4d5f9-816b-4b4a-92b1-fe70c908e7bf .

camssa:6d656d97-4ffe-49e1-a855-b732e7ca2af7 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'EN 16234-1 e-CF provides a common European description of the knowledge, skills and competences of the ICT professional workforce in all sectors. It contributes to moving towards a comprehensive framework for ICT practitioners integrating different building blocks relying not only on the EN 16234-1 e-CF but also on the e-CF User Guide (EN 16234-2) and the e-CF Methodologies (EN 16234-3).\r
\r
CEN reference, CEN/TC 428 - ICT Professionalism and Digital Competences (Published standards), EN 16234-1 e-CF:\r
https://standards.cencenelec.eu/dyn/www/f?p=205:32:0::::FSP_ORG_ID,FSP_LANG_ID:1218399,25&cs=1C0456547BD827F5A977AD2936836C8DA\r
\r
CEN reference, CEN/TC 428 - ICT Professionalism and Digital Competences (Published standards), EN 16234-2 User Guide:\r
https://standards.cencenelec.eu/dyn/www/f?p=CEN:110:0::::FSP_PROJECT,FSP_ORG_ID:70377,1218399&cs=13178F0C970F6172F57335317C3456DD8\r
\r
CEN reference, CEN/TC 428 - ICT Professionalism and Digital Competences (Published standards), EN 16234-3 Methodology:\r
https://standards.cencenelec.eu/dyn/www/f?p=CEN:110:0::::FSP_PROJECT,FSP_ORG_ID:70378,1218399&cs=192D0AD053B765C617E43CC16AD1D4019'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:31b7aef3-b08b-4c31-adbe-0cd0bc8f9fff .

