camssa:7a081165-b474-4123-b438-2ceef9aa023c a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """RDF is included in 4 national catalogues of recommended specifications. They belong to the Netherlands, Slovakia, Spain, and Sweden. The National Interoperability Framework (NIF) of these Member States is fully aligned with at least 4 out of 5 sections of the European Interoperability Framework (EIF) according to the National Interoperability Framework Observatory (NIFO) factsheets.\r
See:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/common-assessment-method-standards-and-specifications-camss/camss-list-standards\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/collection/national-interoperability-framework-observatory-nifo/nifo-factsheets"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:9d877964-3c71-4f21-b162-b4843d41a0ca .

camssa:7a1b3158-58a5-4308-aa48-82429f049c8a a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'ODRL is a policy expression language that provides a flexible and interoperable information model, vocabulary, and encoding mechanisms for representing statements about the usage of content and services.\r
\r
This fact is a key aspect for the interaction and the collaboration between parties and how they need to consume content or services.\r
\r
W3C specification reference:\r
https://www.w3.org/TR/odrl-vocab/#vocab-core'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:be34eb47-c140-45b8-aa83-f3da4db9a06b .

camssa:7a1be273-2da7-4ee2-8f72-f3697f9e3191 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The specification was developed in 2001, by OASIS. It has been evolved over the years demonstrating that it overcame the possible issues raised on the developement and implementation. Moreover, the version of the issue being assessed is the 3.0, which enforces the maturity of the spec for its use in the developement of products and services. Furthermore, the XACML specification has matured enough to be supported in a growing number of commercial identity management products.

XACML OASIS reference:
https://docs.oasis-open.org/xacml/3.0/xacml-3.0-core-spec-os-en.html#_Toc325047066

XACML coverpages reference:
https://xml.coverpages.org/xacml.html'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:6115eb0b-8f81-422f-ac10-a35e84fadfc8 .

camssa:7a1ece86-2815-42a6-a389-14a22e34de78 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-3dd3a1fb1ac7d78d162dab36230e70a34459f38a795e765cc5fdd304c8c04907 ;
    cav:value "60" .

camssa:7a1f1550-ccba-4d7d-b434-dbc87365bf47 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'DPV has been found to contribute to the development of the SPECIAL project (Scalable Policy-aware Linked Data Architecture For Privacy, Transparency and Compliance), a European-funded project that aims to advance the state of the art by delivering technology to reconcile the contradiction between Big Data innovation and data protection compliance requirements.

DPV specification:
https://w3c.github.io/dpv/primer/

SPECIAL project:
https://specialprivacy.ercim.eu/'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4fc33271-454d-478e-80e7-b3fcb710a84a .

camssa:7a2502dc-c1bf-4408-86ff-c56570723381 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-0c9dd43038136296b52f50e05f9d6eef39f7b8018517224007990b15be2eb19e ;
    cav:value "0" .

camssa:7a253e8e-dcce-47bb-af83-84c67dee095a a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'FTP is a protocol exclusively designed for the transfer of files which does not secure the traffic.\r
\r
However,  there are existing extensions of FTP that secure the information delivery. For instance, FTP over TLS, known as FTPS, uses the Transport Layer Security (TLS) to secure FTP communications over a network.\r
\r
FTP Specification Reference:\r
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc959\r
\r
Securing FTP with TLS Reference:\r
https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4217'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:3c5e9940-1f61-4ad8-a5ad-4ac49bd65cbc .

camssa:7a36818d-8e90-4eb3-bcaa-b4b9df5e5f5c a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'UTF-8 itself does not allow customization, but it can be used as a basis for custom encodings or variations that include additional characters or provide optimizations for specific use cases. However, using custom encodings may create interoperability issues with other systems that use standard UTF-8 encoding.\r
\r
UTF-8 specification:\r
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc3629\r
'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:996685c6-af03-437f-a79f-4d5c44420f92 .

camssa:7a37a94b-8ca6-427a-91ad-5671e5b21e96 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-3aec34db3622bf18675b8216d0fc1d68fc7eeb501b4f3326402038a3ceb95c21 ;
    cav:value "1" .

camssa:7a37b59b-4db0-47af-b5a9-1c44e750ca2b a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The IETF is a consensus-based group, and authority to act on behalf of the community requires a high degree of consensus and the continued consent of the community. The process of creating an Internet Standard is straightforward: a specification undergoes a period of development and several iterations of review by the Internet community and revision based upon experience, is adopted as a Standard by the appropriate body... and is published. In practice, the process is more complicated, due to (1) the difficulty of creating specifications of high technical quality; (2) the need to consider the interests of all of the affected parties; (3) the importance of establishing widespread community consensus; and (4) the difficulty of evaluating the utility of a particular specification for the Internet community. The goals of the Internet Standards Process are:_x000D_\r
- technical excellence_x000D_\r
- prior implementation and testing;_x000D_\r
- clear, concise, and easily understood documentation;_x000D_\r
- openness and fairness; and _x000D_\r
- timeliness_x000D_\r
The goal of technical competence, the requirement for prior implementation and testing, and the need to allow all interested parties to comment all require significant time and effort. The Internet Standards Process is intended to balance these conflicting goals. The process is believed to be as short and simple as possible without sacrificing technical excellence, thorough testing before adoption of a standard, or openness and fairness._x000D_\r
See:https://www.ietf.org/standards/process/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:14495dc9-3f73-4897-bf33-12e69f1cae0a .

camssa:7a38dc68-ccc6-4bef-82ce-1b45b23e087f a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'NUTS is included in the Taxonomies EU Vocabularies repository of standards.\r
\r
Publications Office, EU Vocabularies repository:\r
https://op.europa.eu/en/web/eu-vocabularies/taxonomies'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:34309d93-2f2b-4fc8-8144-67c538870c8d .

camssa:7a3c7380-bb16-4792-b806-6f6f6654fd69 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement "At the moment of performing the assessment, UTF-8 is widely adopted in environments developing and implementing ICT solutions including innovative solutions. "@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:89ebdc26-b5aa-4521-a29d-eb55f035144e .

camssa:7a427897-a3a2-4d0c-b80b-f9487b9c58af a cav:Score,
