camssa:7e35f62d-057f-463b-9c16-ef2b144546c2 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'IPsec can be customised in the sense that the user might opt for one configuration or the other depending on the host implementation requirements needed to deploy the specification. For example it is possible to modify the IPsec tunnels created by the IPsec wizard or convert them into custom tunnels to access more options. 

IPsec tunnels customisation:
https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.0/ssl-vpn-to-ipsec-vpn-migration/137787/part-2-configuring-ipsec-tunnels-using-the-vpn-wizard

IPSec reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6071.html'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:277703c7-756c-4069-89b6-f9954316f5c8 .

camssa:7e38ba7f-4e65-4a74-9d9a-cf2d523eb481 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The TrainingDML-AI Standard enables data portability by defining a model and encodings for exchanging and retrieving training data via the Web. This could potentially support the implementation or evolution of European public services that use AI/ML, by enabling the standardized exchange of training data between different systems and applications.

OGC TrainingDML-AI Standard:
https://docs.ogc.org/is/23-008r3/23-008r3.html'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:1d7ee366-0410-4bb6-86dd-90b3f1ef4701 .

camssa:7e3b5090-5c88-42e3-98de-fd6d76940362 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'DNS over HTTPS (DoH) is designed to be technology agnostic meaning that it operates at the application layer of the networking stack and can be implemented across different platforms and technologies. DoH focuses on securing the communication between clients (such as web browsers or applications) and DNS resolvers by encrypting DNS queries using the HTTPS protocol.

DoH specification: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8484'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:e69a3adf-fccf-4a0e-a542-b187a3d5a5dc .

camssa:7e3b5b45-bed4-495c-bbc5-cb4e4cd41b55 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-7d28663fdb5deb361d5ad773d7b7ece3c6acf8d2eb7a1222a60cb08df4c93aac ;
    cav:value "0" .

camssa:7e437007-4f1c-4f01-9dcd-e3d5e521bd1f a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The rules on European standardisation allow the European Commission to identify information and communication technology (ICT) technical specifications - that are not national, European or international standards - to be eligible for referencing in public procurement. This allows public authorities to make use of the full range of specifications when buying IT hardware, software and services, allowing for more competition in the field and reducing the risk of lock-in to proprietary systems.\r
\r
After being evaluated compliant with the regulation on standardisation 1025/2012, HTTP has been identified by Commission Implementing Decision and included in the European list of ICT Standards for e-procurement.\r
\r
See: \r
https://ec.europa.eu/growth/industry/policy/ict-standardisation/ict-technical-specifications_en """@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:82bccf59-18b9-4fd6-b843-1b77c59fa14b .

camssa:7e46fafd-cf62-4390-b104-7ff429d4c119 a cav:Score,
