    cav:value "100" .

camssa:869b234f-2851-4ad0-8eaa-1045ea39d018 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'XBRL GL is machine readable data and as a interface format and does not require special software. XBRL is\r
based on XML which is under the W3C.\r
XBRL GL has the semantic information of XBRL GL, which can be used to drive Linked Data. The\r
specification includes URI references extensively.\r
While the primary representation of XBRL GL is with the syntax of XBRL, the semantic is reusable in many\r
syntaxes, and unique in its design. XBRL worked closely with the W3C in evaluating the XBRL syntax and\r
W3C specifications (https://www.w3.org/2009/02/xbrl-ig/charter.html), noting “Facts, concepts and links in\r
XBRL documents can be readily mapped into the Semantic Web. XBRL concepts can be identified via the\r
XML namespace for the schema document defining them as elements together with the element name for\r
each concept.”\r
Related to the levels of 5 star data:\r
“make your stuff available on the Web (whatever format) under an open license”\r
XBRL GL is freely licensed and royalty free\r
“make it available as structured data (e.g., Excel instead of image scan of a table)”\r
XBRL GL is a specification for structuring and expressing business data at any level of detail or aggregation\r
“make it available in a non-proprietary open format (e.g., CSV instead of Excel)”\r
XBRL is a non-proprietary open format. XBRL also can be expressed using JSON, CSV, and other formats.\r
“use URIs to denote things, so that people can point at your stuff”\r
While XBRL GL’s primary use is within the Enterprise and for data kept confidential by government\r
agencies, such as tax filings, URIs are used internally, and the W3C has noted the ease of mapping XBRL\r
syntax documents into Linked Data environments.\r
“link your data to other data to provide context”\r
XBRL GL has been uniquely designed to link both to source data and other contextual information at the\r
detail level, and to unambiguously link to summary reporting information at the aggregate level. The SRCD\r
portion of the Specification formalizes the summarization of XBRL GL detail to other reported open data. The\r
specifications for relating to underlying source information are internally being harmonized with other open\r
specifications for relating to other information, as part of other interoperability efforts under UN/CEFACT and\r
the ISO.\r
\r
In this case, XBRL GL is a data representation schema that is based on XML; XBRL GL makes data available in a structured manner aligning with the second-star from Tim Berners-Lee approach. Therefore, the specification facilitates the publication of open data.\r
\r
About XBRL ( XBRL GL is based on) and linked data in general: \r
https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.232.971&rep=rep1&type=pdf\r
'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:24f0841e-3ff6-4c6f-8c8c-ab60ec5f646b .

camssa:869b60a8-87e6-40d9-92bb-76c2ec72e3dc a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-583a0f52df591048a41be40620830a70260e77855364174d91ba159970d8488e ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:869c39a7-b368-42c2-9713-8e2f2fcda13e a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'While the CSIP itself does not specifically facilitate organizational interoperability agreements, its implementation can indirectly support interoperability efforts by promoting standardized formats, metadata standardization, compliance with standards and regulations, and the development of data exchange guidelines.\r
\r
E-ARK CSIP specification:\r
https://earkcsip.dilcis.eu/#introduction'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:bffa7897-2fd9-4ad2-bc9b-e29b6eb14192 .

camssa:869d3f21-4bc8-409c-9a7c-bde7ce29b528 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """IETF is a consensus-based group, and authority to act on behalf of the community requires a high degree of consensus and the continued consent of the community. The process of creating and Internet Standard is straightforward: a specification undergoes a period of development and several iterations of review by the Internet community and revision based upon experience, is adopted as a Standard by the appropriate body... and is published. In practice, the process is more complicated, due to (1) the difficulty of creating specifications of high technical quality; (2) the need to consider the interests of all of the affected parties; (3) the importance of establishing widespread community consensus; and (4) the difficulty of evaluating the utility of a particular specification for the Internet community. The goals of the Internet Standards Process are:\r
- Technical excellence;\r
- prior implementation and testing;\r
- clear, concise, and easily understood documentation;\r
- openness and fairness; and \r
- timeliness.\r
The goal of technical competence, the requirement for prior implementation and testing, and the need to allow all interested parties to comment all require significant time and effort. The Internet Standards Process is intended to balance these conflicting goals. The process is believed to be as short and simple as possible without sacrificing technical excellence, thorough testing before adoption of a standard, or openness and fairness.\r
\r
See:\r
https://www.ietf.org/standards/process/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:18dde53e-4fcb-4fa2-a45c-ff46931fb271 .

