camssa:979bd4d1-c4c0-4164-b624-a67182839856 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Like all the IETF standards, this specification is a free and open technical specification, built on IETF standards and licenses from the Open Web Foundation. Therefore it is licensed on a royalty-free basis.\r
No IPR disclosures have been submitted directly on this RFC.\r
\r
Intellectual Property Rights in IETF:\r
https://tools.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8179'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:720180ab-59fb-4093-be8f-6df14da0207d .

camssa:97a0869b-860a-4f56-b3dc-68d3bf0b7d30 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """"The specification's namespaces terms make sure to identify the language they are working with, but it does say anything about any multilingual context.\r
\r
DCMI About:\r
https://www.dublincore.org/about/ \""""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:29490045-3efa-4cf5-ae01-503824f1ec00 .

camssa:97a0e8df-fef4-4323-be4e-73eb1ffbbc0d a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """Ogg started as a simple project of audio compression in 1993. In 2003, two Internet RFCs were published relating to the Ogg format. The Ogg bitstream published as RFC 3533 and RFC 3534 which includes the Internet content type of Ogg. It became very popular for being an open source multimedia container format. So it is mature enough for its use in the development of products and services.\r
\r
Ogg XIPH reference:\r
https://xiph.org/ogg/doc/rfc3533.txt"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:65a4a4e5-f3a9-4316-ae81-106353e18ffe .

