camssa:a1252604-c4a9-4e2e-8184-0ad58446cc9f a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'ODRL specification itself is primarily focused on expressing and managing digital rights for content and does \r
not directly address e-accessibility concerns. Nevertheless, implementing ODRL can still take steps to \r
ensure that the systems and content are accessible. This may incorporate accessibility features into user \r
interfaces, ensuring compatibility with assistive technologies, and following best practices for accessible \r
design.\r
W3C specification reference: \r
https://www.w3.org/TR/odrl-vocab/#vocab-core'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4dc0245b-15ab-4f2f-9723-d2b5d69ef261 .

camssa:a126d972-9039-44fe-8e85-df7b98861613 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-a2e2820561158593079cf28230b73b9b7f1cb83da49b04d2317790e20c0c706f ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:a1292e18-b200-44b8-b51f-4f1376efc907 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'XSLT demonstrates market acceptance through extensive testing, proving its reliability and maturity. Its design prioritises interoperability with standards like HTML, XPath, and XDM, allowing seamless integration into digital solutions. It also efficiently processes large datasets via streaming and supports formats like JSON.

There are multiple real use cases where XSLT is used, for example, one scenario can be EFT/EDI transformations using XSLT involve processing large, denormalised XML extractions for tasks like nested regrouping, sorting, and calculations to produce plain text output. 

Real use cases of XSLT:
https://www.w3.org/TR/xslt-21-requirements/#scenarios

XSLT References:
https://www.w3.org/TR/xslt-30/#xml-versions
https://www.w3.org/TR/xslt-30/#streaming'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:21660e1b-21a5-4466-8b12-c5eb0a45454d .

camssa:a12b7cea-42d6-47bd-91af-a9faba4e0cd5 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The Core Public Organisation Vocabulary was released in December 2016. Its first aspiration was to become a common data model for describing public organisations in the European Union. So, CPOV is mature enough for its use in the development of products and services.\r
\r
CPOV Joinup reference:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/solution/e-government-core-vocabularies/core-public-organisation-vocabulary"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:b3e8140d-447e-486e-bae2-079b36116915 .

camssa:a12d4042-e86f-4fda-9d1d-ff8ef82d7a6b a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'To prevent unauthorised changes, TLS 1.3 employs Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) algorithms. These symmetric encryption algorithms provide both confidentiality and integrity protection. Every data record is authenticated, enabling detection of any tampering. This per-record authentication ensures robust protection against unauthorised changes.

TLS 1.3 Record Layer Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#appendix-E.2'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:515a70f0-f0be-4014-9b13-c1a3a7e74d2b .

camssa:a13008c8-aa0a-4c2d-99ee-f865f789cd8d a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-9694ffda6be83e507b6d3395cb411581d91419db0107726873a0a9018eab6a54 ;
    cav:value "20" .

camssa:a130953b-6fa3-4231-8b93-9e239b22fe2f a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'DNS over HTTPS (DoH) usability extends beyond a specific business domain, and it can be employed across various sectors and contexts. DoH can be used by individuals, organisations, and businesses across different industries for securing DNS traffic. Any entity, regardless of the business domain, can benefit from using DoH to protect sensitive information and prevent eavesdropping on DNS traffic.

DoH specification: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8484'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:bbb36b91-3547-4c06-a600-da83482f1297 .

