camssa:cc1b0056-8d45-4dda-bce7-555b0e458c8e a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The Registered Organization Vocabulary is a simplified, reusable and extensible data model that captures the fundamental characteristics of a legal entity. It facilitates the information exchange between business sectors. So, it fosters data portability between systems and applications supporting the implementation and evolution of European public services.\r
\r
RegOrg Joinup reference:\r
https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/solution/registered-organization-vocabulary\r
\r
RegOrg W3C reference:\r
https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-regorg/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:6509ded7-3b06-40ed-a6bf-84b78f1540a6 .

camssa:cc1da55c-4dd9-4645-a93e-8c255ee1d237 a cav:Score,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:assignedTo sc:c-549e39fc635763b9466e09688714b2908fcf7a349d4f4f0fe2a761bd6f502733 ;
    cav:value "100" .

camssa:cc27cfad-5a7c-4731-9f70-da51fe18a94f a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """The IETF is a consensus-based group, and authority to act on behalf of the community requires a high degree of consensus and the continued consent of the community. The process of creating and Internet Standard is straightforward: a specification undergoes a period of development and several iterations of review by the Internet community and revision based upon experience, is adopted as a Standard by the appropriate body... and is published. In practice, the process is more complicated, due to (1) the difficulty of creating specifications of high technical quality; (2) the need to consider the interests of all of the affected parties; (3) the importance of establishing widespread community consensus; and (4) the difficulty of evaluating the utility of a particular specification for the Internet community. The goals of the Internet Standards Process are:\r
- Technical excellence;\r
- prior implementation and testing;\r
- clear, concise, and easily understood documentation;\r
- openness and fairness; and \r
- timeliness.\r
The goal of technical competence, the requirement for prior implementation and testing, and the need to allow all interested parties to comment all require significant time and effort. The Internet Standards Process is intended to balance these conflicting goals. The process is believed to be as short and simple as possible without sacrificing technical excellence, thorough testing before adoption of a standard, or openness and fairness.\r
\r
See:\r
https://www.ietf.org/standards/process/"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:68ae487c-4a5b-4947-9743-e3d7fcf16745 .

camssa:cc28ba4a-68e6-42be-8ea4-9df17fb286b1 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'Domibus has been found being used at the core of the Multilingual Anonymisation for Public Administrations (MAPA) project, which aims at introducing NLP tools and at developing a toolkit for effective and reliable anonymisation of tect on the medical and legal fields.\r
\r
MAPA project:\r
https://mapa-project.eu/2022/01/\r
\r
Domibus specification:\r
https://ec.europa.eu/digital-building-blocks/wikis/display/DIGITAL/Domibus\r
\r
Regulation (EU) 910/2014:\r
https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=uriserv%3AOJ.L_.2014.257.01.0073.01.ENG'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:9da5605d-324f-4656-afa0-78bd87d8d33c .

camssa:cc2b8ec8-cf02-401c-9903-21a09e512d05 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """'The ETSI TS 119 612 specification establishes security mechanisms, such as digital signatures, to guarantee the authentication of agents involved in data transactions. In this context, it ensures the authenticity of agents by signing a trusted list.

ETSI TS 119 612:
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/119600_119699/119612/02.02.01_60/ts_119612v020201p.pdf'"""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:4f46361e-2da4-4cf0-bdfd-36a9383efe74 .

