camssa:d0e61a36-42fb-4dde-a6ab-0aa8f3a46739 a cav:Statement,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    cav:judgement """"TLS 1.3 offers a foundational level of access control by focusing on peer authentication. This means it verifies the identities of the server and, optionally, the client, ensuring they are who they claim to be. This verification is done using certificates (digital documents issued by trusted authorities that link a public key to an identity) or pre-shared keys (PSKs) (secret keys shared beforehand between the communicating parties).

It's important to know that these mechanisms are for authentication only, confirming who is communicating, and not for data encryption. Therefore, TLS 1.3 itself does not control what actions a user can perform or what resources they can access.

TLS 1.3 Resumption and Pre-Shared Key Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#section-4.2.9

TLS 1.3 Certificates and Authentication Reference:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#appendix-C.2\""""@en ;
    cav:refersTo camssa:8d0b2206-47d7-49c2-98a0-83669b01dedd .

